"top": [[" driver", -0.39486467838287354], [" drivers", -1.1866203546524048]]}, {"t": " discussions", "lp": -0.7515082359313965, "top": [[" discussions", -0.7515082359313965], [",", -0.9458937644958496]]}, {"t": ",", "lp": -0.006840985268354416, "top": [[",", -0.006840985268354416], [" about", -5.833905220031738]]}, {"t": " it", "lp": -0.9766144156455994, "top": [[" it", -0.9766144156455994], [" they", -1.1693062782287598]]}, {"t": " most", "lp": -1.0566551685333252, "top": [[" most", -1.0566551685333252], [" almost", -1.2040646076202393]]}, {"t": " commonly", "lp": -0.054507140070199966, "top": [[" commonly", -0.054507140070199966], [" often", -2.952376365661621]]}], "text": "Based on current technical documentation and driver behavior for Intel Arc GPUs (including the A770) under the Linux `xe` driver (or the newer `intel-mesa`/`i915` drivers), here is the breakdown:\n\n### 1. **CCS Mode 1 vs. Mode 2**\n\n**CCS (Constant Color Surface)** modes refer to how the GPU handles **color space conversions** and **gamma correction** during rendering, particularly when using **YUV** or **HDR** content.\n\n- **CCS Mode 1**:  \n  - Typically corresponds to **standard sRGB** or **linear RGB** handling with **no additional gamma correction** applied by the driver.  \n  - Used for **SDR (Standard Dynamic Range)** content where the display pipeline expects already-corrected sRGB values.  \n  - Common for desktop compositing, video playback of standard content, and games that output sRGB directly.\n\n- **CCS Mode 2**:  \n  - Typically corresponds to **HDR (High Dynamic Range)** or **extended color gamut** handling.  \n  - Applies **additional gamma correction** or **tone mapping** to convert HDR values (e.g., BT.2021, Display-P3) to the display\u2019s native color space.  \n  - Used when rendering **HDR content** (e.g., HDR video, HDR games) or when the application outputs linear HDR values that need to be tone-mapped for display.\n\n> **Note:** The exact mapping of \"Mode 1\" vs. \"Mode 2\" can vary slightly depending on the driver version and kernel version. In some contexts, CCS modes may also refer to **compression modes** for constant-color surfaces (e.g., solid-color fills), but in the context of Intel Arc and `xe` driver discussions, it most commonly"}